analysisWe need to read the comment: 

```java
// @@@ If the starting index is greater than or equal to -1, then for every index greater than the result and less than or equal to the starting index, the bit at that index is set to true.
```

We need to produce an assert statement reflecting this specification. The method is previousClearBit(int fromIndex). It returns the index of the previous clear bit (i.e., false) before or at fromIndex. The comment says: If the starting index is >= -1, then for every index greater than the result and <= starting index, the bit at that index is set to true. So for all i such that result < i && i <= fromIndex, get(i) == true. Also note that the method may return -1 when no clear bit before fromIndex (including fromIndex) exists. The condition "starting index is greater than or equal to -1" is basically the precondition for method (since it throws exception if fromIndex < -1). So we need to assert that if fromIndex >= -1 then for all i with \result < i && i <= fromIndex, this.get(i) is true.

Edge: If result == -1, then condition "result < i && i <= fromIndex" becomes -1 < i && i <= fromIndex, i.e., i in [0, fromIndex] (if fromIndex >=0). The spec says bits in that range are true. That matches semantics: if no clear bit, all bits up to fromIndex are true.

Thus assert: (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; this.get(i));

But this.get(i) returns boolean; we need to assert it's true. So we can write this.get(i) == true, or simply this.get(i). In JML, boolean expression is okay. Use "this.get(i)" as predicate.

Thus final assert: assert (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; this.get(i));.

Make sure syntax: \forall int i; condition; spec. Use parentheses around implication maybe.

Thus final answer in code block.

assistantfinal<code>assert (fromIndex >= -1) => (\forall int i; \result < i && i <= fromIndex; this.get(i));</code>
